# Intro
One 29.7 Msun star in an isolated, dust-free box of cold (50 K), H2-poor gas, with the ISRF
transport on (`ISRF_propagation: 1`). The star's Lyman-Werner (LW) light dissociates the H2 in
the box. The box is thin: the H2 column is too small for the lines to absorb a visible part
of the light. The check compares the star's luminosity with the radiation table and the
H2 dissociation rate with the field that the cooling is given.

The run uses geajay cooling with `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1` and
`ISRF_packet_light_speed: unlimited` (one hop per step). Official Grackle refuses the
line absorption.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
make -j
```
The radiation table must carry the `Data/Radiation` group; see the README of
`ISRFH2Photodissociation` for how to obtain it.

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
`level` (resolution, N = (2^level)^3 gas particles), `gas_density`, `gas_mass`, `star_mass`,
`n_threads` and `force=1` (remove an existing `snap/` and `thin/`)
override the defaults. The run takes a few minutes on 4 threads and writes a snapshot every
1e-10 time units (about 0.1 yr) to `thin/snap`. `run.sh` prints the command of the check.

# Check
```
python3 check_h2_thin_absolute.py thin/snap --radiation-table PopII_parsec_spectral.hdf5
```
Three tests gate the run, and the exit status is 0 when all pass:
- the star's `LWLuminosities` against the luminosity that SWIFT reads from the radiation
  table for the star's mass and metallicity;
- the rate identity: the H2 dissociation rate of every particle equals the LW field that the
  cooling is given, times the Draine-field rate coefficient, in each radial shell;
- the stored light `Q` (see below) in three radial shells against a two-sided bar. The check
  computes the bar itself: it propagates the light through the gas of the run with a model of
  the transport, using the cone power and the star smoothing length of the snapshots, and takes
  the expected `Q` in each shell with a margin of 3 standard deviations over random emission
  frames. The check takes about one minute. It needs scipy. The bar is gated at one
  reception slot count only; other counts are untested. It applies to `level=5` and `star_mass=29.7`; with other values
  this test prints `VOID` and the other tests still run.

The check also prints the LW energy density in each shell over the thin-limit
value `L / (4 pi r^2 c)` (`Q`) and the dissociation rate over the thin-limit kernel mean,
both without a bar, and the `Q` of the three bar shells with its bar.
Exit status 1 is a failed test (any test, also when the `Q` bar is void), 2 a missing file, 3 a value that is not finite, 4 a void `Q` bar with no failed test, or a run that
does not meet the conditions of the check (one star, no dust, no periodic images, unlimited
light speed).
